&lt;p&gt;Please join the Ukrainian Studies Program at the Harriman Institute for a poetry reading and discussion with &lt;strong&gt;Sasha Dugdale&lt;/strong&gt; and&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;Oksana Maksymchuk&lt;/strong&gt;. Moderated by&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://harriman.columbia.edu/person/mark-andryczyk/"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Mark Andryczyk&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Contemporary poetry can challenge, explore uncomfortable truths, and bear witness to unimaginable experiences. In Sasha Dugdale&amp;rsquo;s new collection, &amp;quot;The Strongbox,&amp;quot; recent history and Greek mythology meet. Her varied cast of characters are abducted to foreign lands, travel through war zones, and are haunted by conflicts. Oksana Maksymchuk&amp;rsquo;s &amp;quot;Still City&amp;quot; reflects life in the wake of extreme and unpredictable violence. Beginning as a poetic journal kept from her hometime in Ukraine, 2021-22, it chronicles events as the poet, her family and community prepare for airstrikes, as well as nuclear, chemical and biological warfare. Together, Oksana and Sasha discuss what it is to write poetry in these times of conflict.&lt;/p&gt;

