&lt;p&gt;Please join the Program on U.S.-Russia Relations for a talk by&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;Paul Stephan&lt;/strong&gt;. Moderated by&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;Elise Giuliano&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Russia&amp;rsquo;s invasion of Ukraine in February 2022 provoked fury and broad condemnation. It seems as clear an instance of evil versus good and international lawlessness in international relations as we have seen in some time. Yet Russia has not been repulsed, even if it has not achieved its objectives, and the economic sanctions imposed by the West produced mostly short-term pain. The ambiguity of the Trump administration&amp;rsquo;s position on the war certainly has not helped. Where are the consequences of this outrageous act?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The answer is that Russia has done terrible, perhaps irreversible harm to its position as a regional power. It will face a geopolitical and geoeconomic reckoning, although it may take five to ten years for the real costs to be manifest. This talk will give an account of this reckoning and will reflect&amp;nbsp;more generally on the efficacy of international law and the liberal international order as a means of disciplining powerful states.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Courageous Conversations&lt;/strong&gt; is an interactive workshop designed to help participants engage in honest, respectful, and authentic conversations across differences. Developed by &lt;a href="https://leadershape.org/"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;LeaderShape&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;, Inc., it provides an opportunity to explore challenging topics, deepen self-awareness, and practice navigating difficult conversations with care and courage.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Through guided reflection, structured dialogue exercises, and small-group learning, you&amp;rsquo;ll build the skills to approach complex conversations thoughtfully, lean into discomfort, and use dialogue as a tool for personal growth and stronger connections with others.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This session is a part of the &lt;a href="https://universitylife.columbia.edu/CCI"&gt;Community Citizenship Initiative&lt;/a&gt; (CCI) and is designed for first semester students who want to develop dialogue skills to better communicate across our differences. Students attending this workshop will receive credit for CCI and will have their requirement considered complete.&lt;/p&gt;
